WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our next guest is
the award-winning host

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.936
of "The Graham Norton Show."

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.971
The second season of his show,
"Queen of the Universe,"

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:08.674
will premiere on June 2nd
on Paramount Plus.

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:11.143
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend from across the pond,

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.546
Graham Norton, everybody!

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:17.817
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:25.791 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:27.360
-Welcome.
-Thank you very much.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.996
-Welcome, welcome, welcome.
This is a big deal for us.

00:00:30.996 --> 00:00:33.032
-Wow. That's -- Yeah, that's --

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:34.267
[ Cheering ]

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:38.004
It's impressive
that they can cheer

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:40.006
and whoop like that
while Googling me.

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:41.507
-Oh, no, no, no.

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:43.576
See, you don't realize
you're a big deal over here

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:45.345
and people are very excited.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.845
[ Cheering ]

00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:49.415
-I think a lot of them think
they would have to

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.484
cross the Atlantic to see you.
-Take a plane.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:53.052
-And now they get you here
in person.

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:56.189
-Here I am.
-Here you are.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.791
-And I'm very happy to be here.
I love New York City.

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:01.528
I think it's very good
for old people fun.

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:03.596
-Oh, wow. Interesting.
Meaning what?

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.765
-Well, you know,
my days of daytime clubbing

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:07.567
and zip-lining, they're over.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:08.801
-And you were
very good at them both.

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:11.270
-I loved them.
-Were you a zip liner for real?

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.839
-No.
-Okay.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:14.574
-You went to some clubs
in the daytime, though.

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.675
-That I did, too.
-Yeah.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.710
-So now I'm here,

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.345
and, you know,
you can go to see shows.

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:21.113
There's galleries.
They deliver things.

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:23.916
It's easy to get a cab.
It's old people fun.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.952
And if you are
an older thrill-seeker,

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:28.454
you can be excited
by maybe being hit by a bike,

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:31.891
because that gets
your heart rate up.

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:34.060
-That's the new zip-line.
-Yeah, that is it.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.594
-Yeah.
Ooh! Where's my medication?

00:01:35.594 --> 00:01:38.430
-You obviously, you usually
are asking the questions.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:39.765
How do you feel about

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:42.035
being the interviewee
as opposed to the interviewer?

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:45.371
-I mean, not great,
to be honest --

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:46.873
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:50.844
So sorry, apologies in advance.
That bit's so much easier.

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:53.079
It's so much easier to be you.
-I agree.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:55.281
I just did James Corden's
show last week,

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:57.216
and I genuinely said to him,

00:01:57.216 --> 00:02:00.353
"Being the guest
is so much harder."

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.421
-Really, really hard.
So bear that in mind.

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:03.923
[ Cheers ]

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.125
This is very difficult.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:07.527
[ Applause ]

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.262
-And I think that, you know,

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:10.897
sometimes I'll have people,

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:12.599
often will be on the show
and when it's over,

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:14.032
they'll lean over and go, "How
do you do this every night?"

00:02:14.032 --> 00:02:16.102
And I'm like, doing it
every night is the easy part.

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:18.638
Doing it once every six months,
that's the hard part.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.539
-Yeah, no, I remember
when I was growing up,

00:02:20.539 --> 00:02:23.176
there was a show in Ireland
called "The Late Late Show,"

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:26.979
and I mean,
it started about 7:00, but...

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:30.182
but it was dark out, so...

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:32.452
And there was
a host called Gay Byrne.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.920
That was actually his name.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.689
And he did it forever.
The show is still going.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.558
And as a kid, I always dreamt of

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:40.993
being a guest on the show
and it's only as an adult,

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.163
I've realized it's much better
to be the host,

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.264
because you get to be
on every week.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:47.767
And also, you know,

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.568
the chances of you
coming back are higher,

00:02:49.568 --> 00:02:50.803
because --
you know what it's like.

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:52.138
When you started this show,

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:54.073
there were probably guests
you really wanted to get.

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.910
-Now, not so much.
-Yup, exactly.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.110
The longer you do it,
you want it --

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.913
you start repeating people
and other people

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:01.247
sort of get
dropped off the ledger.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.448
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.416
-But the thing
that I'm very impressed with,

00:03:04.416 --> 00:03:07.119
because I do think you have a
harder job as a host than I do,

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:10.456
is you have to navigate
multiple guests out at once.

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:12.492
And it's sometimes
as many as six people.

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.727
Also six very famous people.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.929
Do you get nervous
about that part of it?

00:03:16.929 --> 00:03:18.297
-Well, sometimes.

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:21.967
But I think what's good
about actors is -- no offense,

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:23.869
but sometimes they don't
care about an audience.

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:25.104
-Right.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:27.306
-But they do care
about each other.

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:28.374
-I see.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.876
-So if an actor
tells a funny story,

00:03:30.876 --> 00:03:34.647
you can see someone seething,
kind of like,

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:39.152
"I have a funny story!
I [bleep] myself!"

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:42.488
And so the stakes
get higher and higher.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:45.692
Sometimes we do get, like,
terrible stories,

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.527
like, don't tell that
on television!

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:50.229
-Well, that is fascinating,
because I will say,

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.798
in my head,
when I think of famous people

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.766
and the most embarrassing story
they've told on a chat show,

00:03:54.766 --> 00:03:56.636
it's always on yours.

00:03:56.636 --> 00:03:59.505
-But I think that is
because of the other guests.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:00.873
They're trying to outdo
each other.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.209
And also it is funny to say
something really rude

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.210
in front of Dame Judi Dench
or something.

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.147
-And I do believe she is
someone a perfect example.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.450
She knows the role she plays.
-Yes.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.485
-When someone says something,
she knows to

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.820
lean into the Dame
in those moments.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.923
-Absolutely. "Ooh!"

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:19.926
-Do you ever -- I always,
if I ever get nervous --

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:21.127
I think the longer
I do the show,

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.628
the less I get nervous,
which is wonderful.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:25.497
But it is the sort
of very famous people,

00:04:25.497 --> 00:04:27.700
especially famous people
when I was young,

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.635
and you tend
to get a lot of those.

00:04:29.635 --> 00:04:32.004
Do you get nervous anymore
with people like that?

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:34.039
-I mean, sometimes.

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:35.141
Backstage, we were talking
about Tom Cruise.

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.442
-Yeah.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:37.977
-And Tom Cruise is one
of those people, you know --

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:40.212
Because normally once
they come through that door

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:42.781
and they sit down,
pretty quickly,

00:04:42.781 --> 00:04:45.584
it's just a man in a jacket
or some woman in a frock,

00:04:45.584 --> 00:04:47.120
and that's the end of it.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.123
Tom Cruise, I never get past
the fact that it's Tom Cruise.

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:54.594
He remains Tom Cruise
throughout the interview,

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:57.930
and an audience likes that,
and I enjoy that,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.566
and also, you know,
the other guests, you can tell

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:02.568
they're all a bit starry
about him,

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.537
and there aren't many people
like that.

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.606
That kind of old star system's
kind of gone.

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:08.708
-It's really funny
how you would say,

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.209
"Oh, there's no
movie stars anymore.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:11.977
There's just great actors
and actresses."

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:13.412
And then every now
and then you're like,

00:05:13.412 --> 00:05:14.746
"Oh, I just saw
a [bleep] movie star."

00:05:14.746 --> 00:05:17.349
Like, they come out,
the audience is different.

00:05:17.349 --> 00:05:18.584
It's really, it's a trip.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:22.254
-Like, Jennifer Lopez
is not everywoman.

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:23.889
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:26.058
-It's so funny
that she played a maid.

00:05:26.058 --> 00:05:28.694
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.397
Yeah, right.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:32.998
-When you come on

00:05:32.998 --> 00:05:34.133
another one
of these shows like this,

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.001
do you sort of check out
the green rooms?

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:38.037
Are you measuring them up
compared to yours?

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:41.574
-I have to say, already now,
I have to go back to London

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:43.209
and I may have
to change my ways.

00:05:43.209 --> 00:05:45.845
A handwritten note
from Seth Meyers.

00:05:45.845 --> 00:05:48.114
-I should note I learned
the handwritten notes,

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:49.782
that was a gift given to me.

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:51.550
Jimmy Fallon told me, "Make
sure, when you get this job,

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:52.718
you leave a --"

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:53.786
So I leave a handwritten note
every day. Yeah.

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:54.887
[ Crowd awws ]

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.256
-See, what's sweet is,
I used to do it.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:58.591
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.960
-So this isn't a thing
you didn't know about.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.262
You just stopped.
-I just stopped.

00:06:03.262 --> 00:06:04.730
It's like, very nice of you

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.298
to swing by the dressing room,
say hello.

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:07.333
Don't do that anymore, either.

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:08.901
-You don't even say
hi to the guests?

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:09.936
-What's the other thing
I don't do?

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:12.371
Oh, talk to them afterwards.

00:06:12.371 --> 00:06:15.007
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:16.909
[ Cheering ]
No, no.

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:19.244
If I bump into them
in a corridor, I'll say hello.

00:06:19.244 --> 00:06:20.913
-Oh that's lovely.
-Yeah.

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:22.381
-I mean,
that's above and beyond.

00:06:22.381 --> 00:06:24.683
-Sometimes, though,
I don't recognize them,

00:06:24.683 --> 00:06:26.018
and I'll think,
"They're well dressed."

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.521
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:33.492
-I once saw -- A young person
on the street said hi to me,

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:36.795
and I panicked and I thought
they had been a young actor

00:06:36.795 --> 00:06:38.897
who'd been on the show
and I'd forgotten.

00:06:38.897 --> 00:06:40.332
And we were waiting
for the light.

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:43.636
So I leaned in and I go,
"So what have you been up to?"

00:06:43.636 --> 00:06:46.806
And they looked at me and go,
"Just getting ready for finals."

00:06:46.806 --> 00:06:49.541
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.541 --> 00:06:50.943
I was like, "Cool, cool.

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:53.679
That's a normal question
that a man my age asks."

00:06:53.679 --> 00:06:57.182
-Yeah. "You busy right now?"

00:06:57.182 --> 00:06:58.517
-Hey, I got a lot
more questions.

00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:00.853
You guys, that's Graham Norton!
We'll be right back.

